  "complexity_adjustments": {
    "_note": "Each factor is a fraction of the baseline, applied additively and reported as its own hours delta. Nothing here is folded into baseline_hours.",
    "severity": {
      "Minor": -0.15,
      "Moderate": 0.0,
      "Severe": 0.25
    },
    "presence_state": {
      "present": 0.0,
      "detached": 0.1,
      "missing": 0.15
    },
    "structural_involvement": 0.35,
    "difficult_access": 0.2,
    "aluminium_or_hss": 0.3,
    "ev_high_voltage": 0.25,
    "adas_equipped": 0.15,
    "_note_adjacent": "DISABLED (was threshold 3 / +0.05 per extra part / max +0.20). This had the sign backwards: it made EVERY part dearer as the part count rose, when parts in one repair area SHARE teardown and the marginal labour per part should fall. It also handed the vision model a cost multiplier — 11 detected parts put +20% on all 35 operations, and any over-detection inflated every other line with it. Modelling shared teardown properly (one front-end R&I billed once, not per part) is the real fix and is not implemented; zero is the honest placeholder until it is.",
    "adjacent_components": {
      "threshold": 0,
      "per_extra_part": 0.0,
      "max": 0.0
    },
    "total_cap": 0.75
  },
  "_note_inference_blocked": "Categories that may only be reached by an EXPLICIT part_categories entry, never by the substring fallback in labour_engine.part_category(). These carry the highest baseline hours, so a wrong guess is expensive: 'engine_mounts' is unmapped, matched the substring 'engine', and was billed as mechanical_assembly (15 baseline hours, Rs 16,700). Map such parts by name if you want them costed that way.",
